<!-- ── toke Ecosystem ──────────────────────────────────────── -->

<div class="section-heading"><h2>toke Ecosystem</h2></div>
<p>loke is built entirely within the toke ecosystem. Each piece is designed to work together, and each contributes to loke's core mission of minimising tokens and maximising privacy.</p>

<div class="features">
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>toke</h3>
    <p>The programming language. Designed for minimal token consumption &mdash; a 55-character syntax that LLMs can generate with fewer tokens and higher correctness. loke's core engine is written in toke.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>ooke</h3>
    <p>The web framework. Built on toke, compiles to a single native binary with zero runtime dependencies. loke's HTTP interface and the moke demo run on ooke. No Node.js, no Python, no Docker.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="feature">
    <h3>loke</h3>
    <p>The intelligence layer that ties it all together. Built on toke and ooke, loke leverages libraries like TOON (token-optimised serialisation) and AAAK (compressed context shorthand) to minimise token spend while maximising privacy. Build light, ship fast, keep data local.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="ecosystem-note" style="border-left-color:#7c83fd;">
  <p><strong>toke on ooke &mdash; build light, ship fast.</strong> loke ships as a single native binary compiled from toke. No interpreter, no VM, no garbage collector, no runtime dependencies. Install one file and everything works.</p>
</div>

<div class="ecosystem-note">
  <p>loke is part of the <a href="/ecosystem">toke ecosystem</a>. It is a separate project from toke the language. See the <a href="https://github.com/karwalski/loke">loke GitHub repository</a> for source, releases, and contribution guide.</p>
</div>

</div>

